2. Pretrained weights this project can download
================================================================================

u2net.onnx, u2netp.onnx
  Content:  The U^2-Net authors' published weights, exported to ONNX with the
            BatchNorm layers folded into their preceding convolutions.
  Licence:  Apache License 2.0, inherited from the upstream weights.
  Obtained: https://github.com/danielgatis/rembg/releases/download/v0.0.0/
            The rembg project (MIT) redistributes these files; the weights themselves
            originate from https://github.com/xuebinqin/U-2-Net, whose authors
            distribute them via Google Drive.
  SHA-256:  u2net.onnx   8d10d2f3bb75ae3b6d527c77944fc5e7dcd94b29809d47a739a7a728a912b491
            u2netp.onnx  309c8469258dda742793dce0ebea8e6dd393174f89934733ecc8b14c76f4ddd8
            Both digests are pinned in src/cutoutml/models/download_weights.py and
            verified on download.

u2net.pt, u2netp.pt
  Content:  Derived from the two ONNX graphs above by
            cutoutml.models.u2net.from_onnx, which extracts the folded convolution
            weights and neutralises the BatchNorm layers. Numerically equivalent to the
            source graph to within 1.4e-7 (full) and 1.5e-6 (lite), verified against
            onnxruntime at conversion time.
  Licence:  Apache License 2.0. A format conversion does not change the licence of the
            weights, and each converted file carries its provenance and licence inside
            it, so the attribution cannot be separated from the bytes.
  Record:   models/conversions/u2net.json and u2netp.json are committed copies of that
            same provenance - source digest, parity figure, converter version - so the
            claims above are checkable without the weights themselves being present.
            The weight files are not committed; see docs/decisions/ADR-008.

u2net.pth, u2netp.pth (optional alternative route)
  Content:  The authors' original PyTorch checkpoints.
  Licence:  Apache License 2.0.
  Obtained: HuggingFace mirrors, which are blocked in some networks including the one
            this repository was developed in.

================================================================================
3. Weights this project deliberately does NOT download
================================================================================

BiRefNet checkpoints
  The official BiRefNet code is MIT, but SOME third-party fine-tuned BiRefNet
  checkpoints are published under non-commercial terms. Anyone using a BiRefNet
  checkpoint obtained from a third party is responsible for reading that specific
  file's licence; "BiRefNet is MIT" is true of the code and not necessarily of the
  weights.

  This repository ships none, offers no download URL for them, and its BiRefNet
  reimplementation is not weight-compatible with them in any case - so no
  non-commercial artefact can arrive here by accident.

================================================================================
4. Datasets
================================================================================

No third-party dataset is committed to or required by this repository.

Every checkpoint that THIS repository trains - the CutoutNet tiny/small/base variants,
plus u2net-lite and birefnet if you choose to train them - is trained from scratch on
images synthesised procedurally by
src/cutoutml/datasets/synthetic.py, whose shape and background primitives live in
procedural.py, from a single integer seed. No photographs, no
scraped images, no third-party annotations, and no pretrained initialisation: no ImageNet
backbone, no distillation, no fine-tuning of a downloaded checkpoint. Neither torchvision
nor timm is a dependency. Those weights are MIT with no upstream.

The only dataset artefact committed here is datasets/synthetic-eval.json, which is a
generator configuration plus a content fingerprint - not image data. It and the generator
are MIT.

The optional weights in section 2 are the exception, and it is the one worth reading
carefully. The U^2-Net weights were trained by their authors on DUTS, which is
distributed for research use. Their Apache-2.0 licence covers the weight files; it does
not erase the terms of the data they were fitted to. So a deployment that downloads
u2net.onnx and serves predictions from it is in a different position from one that serves
CutoutNet, even though both files sit in the same directory. That distinction is the
reason this repository trains its own models at all, and the reason
`python -m cutoutml.models.download_weights` prints a licence before it fetches anything.

Pointing the trainer at a real dataset (--dataset-root) inherits that dataset's terms in
the same way. DUTS, DIS5K and AM-2k are all research-use. See docs/licensing.md.

